# Project conventions

- Use uv from the repository root. Sync every member with `uv sync --all-packages`.
- Preserve PEP 420 namespace boundaries: no `__init__.py` in shared `__NAMESPACE__/`.
- Add dependencies to the importing member. Keep the member graph acyclic and update
  `docs/workspace-ledger.toml` when ownership or dependency boundaries change.
- Use typed Python and Loguru. Log stable messages with keyword context, such as
  `logger.info("Operation completed", records=count)`. Applications configure the
  shared custom formatter; library imports never reconfigure sinks.
- `uv run inv check` validates Ruff, ty, and workspace boundaries without editing.
  `uv run inv format` sorts imports and formats code. RTK is the default wrapper;
  pass `--no-rtk` for raw output or environments without RTK.
- Keep tests in their member and run `uv run inv test.all` as separate member suites.
  Run focused suites during development and relevant checks before completion.
- Keep product workflows in application/library code and repository automation in `tasks/`.
- After substantive work, update affected docs and record evidence-backed future
  improvements in `docs/development/next-steps.md`. Deduplicate; include the problem,
  evidence, next action, and completion criteria. Do not invent follow-ups.
- Remove completed backlog entries; record meaningful delivered changes in `CHANGELOG.md`.
- When committing, use Conventional Commits: `type(scope): concise summary`, with an
  optional scope. Use `feat` for features, `fix` for bugs, and an appropriate type for
  other work. Mark breaking changes with `!` or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer and explain
  the migration. See `docs/development/README.md` for examples.
- When available, use `evolve-python-workspace` for architecture changes and closeout.
  Use `dream-project-knowledge` when asked to consolidate accumulated project knowledge.
  These conventions still apply when those skills are not installed.
